-- title:
--   Finiteness of the level-N modular function field over κ(j)
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $N$ with $N \neq 0$ and a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, and write $\kappa =$ `ResidueField A` for its residue field. Inside the field of Laurent series `LaurentSeries κ` consider the element $\tilde\jmath =$ `jqModC κ`, namely $q^{-1}$ times the image in $\kappa$ of the integral power series `jNum` $= E_4^3 \cdot$ `dedekindEtaUnitInv` (so $\tilde\jmath$ is the reduction of the $q$-expansion of the $j$-invariant, with leading term $q^{-1}$), and the element `jqNModC κ N` $=$ `qExpand κ N` $(\tilde\jmath)$ obtained from it by the level-$N$ substitution on $q$. The field `modularFunctionFieldC κ N` is by definition the intermediate field of `LaurentSeries κ` generated over $\kappa$ by these two elements. The theorem asserts that `modularFunctionFieldC κ N` is a finite-dimensional vector space over its intermediate subfield generated over $\kappa$ by the single element $\tilde\jmath$ (viewed as an element of `modularFunctionFieldC κ N` via the membership witness `jqModC_mem`); that is, $[\,C_N : \kappa(\tilde\jmath)\,] < \infty$, with no restriction on the characteristic of $\kappa$.
--
--   This is the modular-equation finiteness statement in reduced form: the level-$N$ function field, presented by the two $q$-expansions $\tilde\jmath$ and $\tilde\jmath_N$, is a finite extension of the rational function field generated by the level-one invariant. It is used throughout the construction of models of modular curves over residue fields, for instance in producing curve models and places realising prescribed evaluations of $q$-expansions.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_finiteDimensional_adjoin_jqModC.lean

